# ws-object-reuse A/B — safe object reuse (per-chunk arena + subtype tp_free)

Branch: `ws/object-reuse` (base `merge/transport`). Image: `fpq-object-reuse`.
Baseline image: `fpq-baseline` (main@7e3d2e6). All runs under
`/tmp/fpq-bench.lock`, one at a time, same host (Ryzen 7 7700, 8 cores, 30 GiB,
3.8 GHz, Python 3.13.5, PostgreSQL 18.6).

The change is client-only: values decoded from the row frame (and the columnar
fallback) are born as private subtypes of `float`/`int`/`str`; each subtype has
a `tp_free` that returns the cell to a size-class free list, and cells come from
one 512 MiB reservation reused across reads. A second read reuses the first
read's cells; past the cap every value falls back to the normal constructor.
`FASTPOSTGRES_OBJ_ARENA=0` disables it at process start.

Because `fpq-baseline` is main and the branch also carries `merge/transport`,
the decisive control is the **same image with the pool off** (`OBJ_ARENA=0`).
Both are recorded below.

## Command — materialized DB-API (row frame / `read_rows`)

The default DB-API result is lazy, so it never builds a Python value per row.
`FASTPOSTGRES_MATERIALIZE=1` restores the eager tuple path, which is the path
this change targets.

```
flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g \
  -e BENCH_GIT_REV=baseline -e FASTPOSTGRES_MATERIALIZE=1 \
  fpq-baseline benchmarks.sh 1000000'

flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g \
  -e BENCH_GIT_REV=object-reuse -e FASTPOSTGRES_MATERIALIZE=1 \
  fpq-object-reuse benchmarks.sh 1000000'
```

